// Pull request author: Varunpoojari
// Change: new rule
// Title: TCC Permissions Reset Via Tccutil
// Sigma ID: 8e937b49-ea64-4f7b-9797-0c59a0a0a5dc
// Status: experimental | Level: medium
// Author: Varun Poojari
// Date: 2026-10-01 | Modified: -
// ATT&CK: privilege-escalation, T1548.006
// Description: Detects the use of tccutil to reset Transparency, Consent, and Control (TCC) permissions for
//   AppleEvents or for all services. macOS infostealers such as Banshee reset AppleEvents
//   permissions to clear previous consent decisions and re-trigger automation prompts that let them
//   control other applications. Investigate the parent process, as legitimate resets are usually
//   performed by administrators or MDM tooling.
// False positives:
//   - Administrators or MDM solutions (e.g. Jamf, Kandji) resetting TCC permissions during troubleshooting
// References:
//   - https://www.loobins.io/binaries/tccutil/
//   - https://research.checkpoint.com/2025/banshee-macos-stealer-that-stole-code-from-macos-xprotect/
// Sigma rule: https://github.com/SigmaHQ/sigma/blob/209cd21a0fc022a39a118163fca79a2184a2e981/rules/macos/process_creation/proc_creation_macos_tccutil_reset_permissions.yml
// License: Detection Rule License (DRL) 1.1 - https://github.com/SigmaHQ/Detection-Rule-License
// Converted by sigma2kql (target: microsoft_xdr, pySigma 1.5.1, pySigma-backend-kusto 1.0.1)
// Validation: passed (Kusto.Language, Defender schema)
// Machine-generated and not tested against real telemetry. Verify before use as a detection.

DeviceProcessEvents
| where FolderPath endswith "/tccutil" and ProcessCommandLine contains " reset " and (ProcessCommandLine contains " AppleEvents" or ProcessCommandLine contains " All")
